'''2.''' In ''coal-mining'', walls of cord-wood piled up crosswise to keep the underground roads open so as to secure ventilation. [Leicestershire, Eng.]

'''abatised, abattised''' (ab′a̤-tist), ''p. a.'' Provided with an abatis.

'''abat-jour''' (a-bä′zhör), ''n.'' [F., any contrivance or apparatus to admit light, or to throw it in a desired direction, as a lamp-shade; < ''abattre'', throw down (see ''abate''), + ''jour'', day, daylight: see ''journal.''] '''1.''' A skylight, or any beveled aperture made in the wall of an apartment or in a roof, for the better admission of light from above.—'''2.''' A sloping, box-like structure, flaring upward and open at the top, attached to a window on the outside, to prevent those within from seeing objects below, or for the purpose of directing light downward into the window.

'''abator''' (a-bā′tor), ''n.'' [Also ''abater;'' < ''abate'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>, ''-or''<sup>2</sup>.] One who or that which abates. {{smaller|Specifically, in ''law:'' (''a'') A person who without right enters into a freehold, on the death of the last possessor, before the heir or devisee. (''b'') An agent or cause by which an abatement is procured. (''c'') One who removes a nuisance. See ''abate, abatement.''}}

